// The core library: `use core` gives the words of def-lang, one topic per file.
//
// A line's shape decides first which def wraps the others: fixed words that
// surround other defs (`api ... runs ... and sends ...`, `is ... ?`), or an `as written`
// sentence that starts the line (`all of ... are ...`). Otherwise order matters: a def
// written earlier is applied last, so it is the outer part of a line. That is why
// `a is 2 + 3 > 4` means `a is ((2 + 3) > 4)`. See How it runs in the README.
// So the files are used loosest first.

// ---- languages
// A word declared with `language NAME` can be written after `def`: the indented
// body below that def is then written in that language. Only Python is part of the
// core; other languages are .def files you use where you need them, like
// `use html`. Declare your own the same way: the Python below
// `language NAME` gets `BODY` (the def's body text), `VAR` (the argument values)
// and `fill(BODY, values)` (puts values where A0, A1, ... are), and returns the
// def's value.

// Python: the body is Python, with `self` (the interpreter) and `a` (the arguments). Built into the interpreter.
// Example: twice 4, with def python twice A0 whose body is return number(A0) * 2
//          → 8 (not checked)
language python

use loops
use questions
use values
use lists
use joined
use logic
use comparison
use math
use text
use time
use numbers
use phrases
